text:
maria: heads up — ran into a weird edge with the TS SDK + tool-calls. Model returned an extra key in a function output (confidence: "high") and our typed parse (zod) threw during streaming. Then a retry appended another partial chunk and the incremental parser ended up in an inconsistent state. Anyone seen this? Looking for a small SDK helper or pattern we can add that handles streaming partial JSON + schema mismatch without sacrificing typed ergonomics.

alex: yeah, saw this at Acme — we ended up with a tiny shim that treats the stream as "assembly first, validate later". Collect chunks until you can JSON.parse the buffer, then run schema validation. If validation fails, return the raw object on a side field instead of throwing (so callers can opt-in to strict mode).

sam: async streaming parser sketch that worked for us:
```js
const buffer = []
for await (const chunk of stream) {
  buffer.push(chunk)
  try {
    const txt = buffer.join('')
    const obj = JSON.parse(txt)
    const parsed = MySchema.parse(obj) // zod/superstruct
    handle(parsed)
    buffer.length = 0
  } catch (e) {
    // JSON.parse failed -> still waiting for more chunks
    // schema parse failed -> fallback handled below
  }
}
```
Note: we also include a timeout window so if JSON never closes we emit a soft error and hand the raw string to the caller for diagnostics.

devex: +1 to a helper API. Proposal: SDK export safeStreamParse(stream, { schema?, strict = false, onPartial }) which: collects incremental chunks, attempts incremental JSON.parse, once full object assembled runs schema.parse if provided. If schema fails and strict=true -> throw, else -> return { value: parsedOrRaw, __extra: leftoverKeys } (so typed callers can still access). Important: attach originalRaw + modelTrace metadata for debugging.

lisa: This feels right directionally. A couple of operational questions: 1) when retries happen we sometimes get duplicate prefix chunks — should the SDK dedupe by sequence id or just reset buffer on retry? 2) do we want to expose a "loose" mode that plumbs unknown fields into a reserved property rather than throwing? Pinging @alex for feasibility and perf implications.

alex: re: retries — include a short header in the stream frames like: {seq: 12, attempt: 3, boundary: true}. On retry boundary, reset buffer. We can make it opt-in so older runtimes don't need to change. About loose mode: yes, return shape { result: T | null, extras: Record<string, unknown> } so TS types can be discriminated.

sam: also watch out for partial JSON inside array outputs (streams that emit successive objects). Our helper should support both single-object streams and NDJSON. Maybe add a mode param: { mode: 'single' | 'ndjson' }.

devex: impl note: add utility parseWithZod(obj, schema) that returns { ok: true, value } | { ok: false, raw, issues } to avoid throwing across async boundaries. Keeps SDK ergonomics nicer.

ci-bot: build #412 passed (tests: 172/172) :white_check_mark:

maria: great context, thanks all. If someone can draft a tiny PR with: safeStreamParse + parseWithZod + a short test for the retry boundary, ping me and i'll run the failing trace through it. I can also add a small sample in ts-sdk README showing the loose-mode pattern.

alex: I'll take the PR. Will add schemaVersion in function-call metadata too so retries can validate compatibility.

lisa: thanks — also add a small note in the console docs about how to interpret __extra in logs.

maria: sweet. ping me @maria when ready and i'll review. :raised_hands:
